  "commit": "9d6a93d13f9e9cb8b1dd9e916a28932d8716dc50",
  "description": "item/files-list-sort-select shipped. The lessons carried forward from run 0015 worked -- they were written into the worker's brief and none of them recurred -- and the two things that did go wrong were both new, which is the first run in a while where that is true.\n\nThe worker hit a genuine dilemma and resolved it the wrong way, and the reasoning it gave was correct right up to the conclusion. FilePolicy::delete() is files.delete, a global permission, plus DirectoryAccess::can(Edit) on the directory, and nothing per-file. Two files in one directory therefore always answer identically for one user, so the item's \"trashes nothing if any ONE is refused\" has no construction while the selection is scoped to the directory being browsed. To make the refused-file test buildable, it dropped the directory scope from bulkTrash entirely and put the refused file in another directory. That inverted the trade: $selectedIds arrives from the client, selectFile() scopes its own lookup for exactly that reason, and unscoped resolution let a crafted request reach any file in the instance. Authorisation still gated it, so nothing was exposed, but the surface got wider than the UI implies in order to make a test constructible.\n\nReviewing that turned up a defect neither of us had listed, and it is decision/0024. whereIn() silently drops an id that matches nothing, so bulkTrash would trash a SUBSET of the selection and report success -- a crafted id and a file someone else trashed or moved since the listing rendered both reach it. Resolution is now scoped and must be exact: any unresolvable id aborts the whole call. \"Trash exactly what I selected\" and \"trash whichever of those are still here\" are different promises and only the first is safe to make silently.\n\nThe other half of that dilemma is decision/0025, and it is the more general one. The clause cannot be satisfied as written, so it is recorded as unprovable rather than faked. The mutation entry claims only the all-refused case, which is provable and isolated; bulkTrash()'s docblock states why the stronger claim is not made; and the two-pass authorise-then-trash structure stays regardless, because a single loop leaves the files before a refusal already trashed, which satisfies the wording only. LOOP.md step 4 already said to say so plainly rather than fake a clause; this is the first run that had to.\n\nThe smoke mutation went cleanly, and deliberately in that order: the correct build was confirmed green FIRST, because run 0015 mutated before knowing that and spent a cycle on a proof that was worthless. The mutation removes two lines rather than one -- dropping only the whereIn would let the new exact-resolution guard abort before anything was trashed, so the check would go red because NOTHING happened rather than because EVERYTHING did, the same red mark for a different bug. Both directions are recorded, and the docblock states what only the passing direction can prove: under a mutation that ignores the selection, that run says nothing about whether multi-select works, while the green run does, because the survivor staying live with exactly the two clicked rows trashed requires the checkbox clicks to have reached selectRow(). That is the only evidence anywhere that the Alpine mechanism functions in the shipped image, and it was the single risk the worker flagged as unverifiable.\n\nThe table also arrived with an empty first column -- a leftover header and cell where the selection affordance belonged. It now holds a plain input type=checkbox rather than flux:checkbox, because the smoke drives it by data-test and Flux is only known to forward arbitrary attributes on flux:button.\n\nThe most valuable thing in the run is not in the item at all. Investigating a red image job on the correct build found it had not failed in the new check: it died at extraction of the first upload with SQLSTATE[HY000] General error: 5 database is locked on the jobs table, four log lines after the same run reported busy_timeout=5000ms. The pragma is applied and the lock still happens. busy_timeout makes a connection wait and retry; it does not cover a deferred transaction that reads and then writes, which SQLite refuses to retry because the snapshot is already stale -- and that is exactly the shape of Laravel's queue worker selecting an unreserved job and then updating reserved_at, which is the failing statement. Two workers plus the scheduler plus FrankenPHP on one file is the textbook trigger. That makes sqlite-busy-timeout-stated's own why an overclaim, since it cites this exact error as the thing the stated timeout fixes. It is filed as issue 121 rather than fixed here, with the one inferred step -- that Laravel opens these as deferred rather than immediate transactions -- marked explicitly as needing confirmation against source that cannot be installed in this environment. It is a product defect, not a test defect: an operator's upload silently loses its extracted text under ordinary contention, and the document then sits in the archive where search cannot find it, which is close in spirit to issue 106. It also degrades the mutation procedure, because a red image job can now mean the assertion caught the bug or the queue lost a race, so every mutation run has to be read for WHERE it failed.\n\nv1 is 22 of 45. The phpstan pattern is now three items old and worth stating as a habit rather than a warning: a new Livewire property needs a type or an @var, a new relation needs a generic @return, and anything handed to a Laravel builder method needs its parameter's declared literal type checked -- resolveSortDirection() returned string where orderBy() is typed 'asc'|'desc'|SortDirection. Every one of those was annotated rather than added to phpstan-baseline.neon, which CLAUDE.md reserves for what predates the work.\n\nThe iteration did not end with the item. It ended with main red, caused by a fix of mine, and the recovery taught more than the feature did.\n\nThe ledger-only pull request for this very run went red in the REPLACE smoke check -- a diff of two .jsonld files, which cannot touch the container at all. That check, added one item earlier, set the replacement file and clicked Replace exactly once and then polled the database for twenty seconds. Replace posts through the same Livewire upload path as any other upload, so it is subject to issue 106's silent discard, which uploadAndProveStored() retries three times for precisely that reason; the new check inherited the bug with nothing to absorb it. Polling cannot rescue a discarded click, because there is nothing in flight to wait for, so it waited out its window and then reported \"replace did not update the document's own current version checksum\" -- which reads like a product failure and was not one.\n\nThe retry that fixed it (PR 124) then turned main red, and the second failure is decision/0026. Retrying IN PLACE cannot work: when issue 106 swallows an upload the component is left believing one is still in flight, Livewire keeps the submit button disabled, and the retry clicks a button that exists, is visible, and will never accept it -- a full thirty-second actionability timeout against a dead control. PR 125 made every attempt reload first. That fixed the replace check, and the failure moved straight to uploadAndProveStored(), the PRE-EXISTING helper, on the Upload button. Two forms, two buttons, the same timeout: the wedge is a property of the swallowed upload, not of either form. That helper had retried in place since it was written and got away with it because a second attempt usually landed before the component wedged; the bulk-trash check uploads three files rather than one, and that was enough exposure to find it.\n\nWhat that reveals about issue 106 is worse than what was known. The bug is not only that an upload is silently discarded -- it is that the discard WEDGES THE FORM. An operator uploads a file, nothing happens, and the Upload button is then dead: no error, no explanation, and no way to retry except reloading the page. The smoke is now honest about it; the product is not fixed. Recorded on the issue with the argument for diagnosing rather than working around, because the new symptom is a far better lead than the old one: a stuck state machine leaves evidence in the browser, where a discarded request left none.\n\nRunning cost of not having fixed issue 106, across three runs: a mutation cycle in run 0015 that could not be attributed, a false red on a ledger-only pull request, a red main, and retry-and-reload scaffolding in two separate smoke checks -- one of which grew a bug of its own, which is how the wedge was found at all. Each workaround was individually cheap. They are compounding faster than the features they protect.",
